# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-1.logic.r1 has "theory_holds_persecution_permitted → not_intentionally_hostile"; mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3 have "theory_holds_persecution_permitted" and "theory_holds_persecution_permitted → not_intentionally_hostile"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the theory maintains that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot harm it" and "the theory cannot be charged with being intentionally hostile to the reception of new truths" are false. Is S1, "A theory which maintains that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm, cannot be charged with being intentionally hostile to the reception of new truths; but we cannot commend the generosity of its dealing with the persons to whom mankind are indebted for them.", true then?
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-1.logic.r1 has "theory_holds_persecution_permitted → P persecuted" and "theory_holds_persecution_permitted"; mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3 have "theory_holds_persecution_permitted → P persecuted"; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps mill-liberty-1.logic.r1, to: Suppose "the theory maintains that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot harm it", "it is permitted that the discoverers of new truths are persecuted (requited by martyrdom, dealt with as the vilest of criminals)", "it is permitted that the discoverers of new truths are persecuted (requited by martyrdom, dealt with as the vilest of criminals)" and "it is permitted that the discoverers of new truths are persecuted (requited by martyrdom, dealt with as the vilest of criminals)" are false. Is S3, "That the authors of such splendid benefits should be requited by martyrdom; that their reward should be to be dealt with as the vilest of criminals, is not, upon this theory, a deplorable error and misfortune, for which humanity should mourn in sackcloth and ashes, but the normal and justifiable state of things.", true then?
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-1.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3 have "defends_treatment → ¬values_benefit"; mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 has "theory_holds_persecution_permitted → defends_treatment" and "defends_treatment → ¬values_benefit"; they differ when only "the theory maintains that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot harm it" is true, which the first allows
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered yes, which keeps mill-liberty-1.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the theory maintains that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot harm it" is true and "the theory's defenders defend this mode of treating benefactors" and "the theory's defenders set much value on the benefit of new truths" are false. Is S5, "People who defend this mode of treating benefactors, cannot be supposed to set much value on the benefit; and I believe this view of the subject is mostly confined to the sort of persons who think that new truths may have been desirable once, but that we have had enough of them now.", true then?
mill-liberty-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-1.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3 have "theory_holds_persecution_permitted → defends_treatment" and "greatest_service ∧ P persecuted → ¬generous_to_benefactors"; mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 has "greatest_service ∧ P persecuted → ¬generous_to_benefactors"; they differ when only "the theory maintains that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot harm it", "discovering to the world a truth that deeply concerns it is as important a service as a human being can render" and "the theory deals generously with the persons to whom mankind are indebted for new truths" are true, which the second allows
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-1.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 have "¬values_benefit"; mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3 has "¬generous_to_benefactors"; they differ when only "the theory deals generously with the persons to whom mankind are indebted for new truths" is true, which the first allows
mill-liberty-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: mill-liberty-1.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.2 put it at S5, "People who defend this mode of treating benefactors, cannot be supposed to set much value on the benefit; and I believe this view of the subject is mostly confined to the sort of persons who think that new truths may have been desirable once, but that we have had enough of them now."; mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3 at S1, "A theory which maintains that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm, cannot be charged with being intentionally hostile to the reception of new truths; but we cannot commend the generosity of its dealing with the persons to whom mankind are indebted for them."
mill-liberty-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps mill-liberty-1.logic.r1.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S5, "People who defend this mode of treating benefactors, cannot be supposed to set much value on the benefit; and I believe this view of the subject is mostly confined to the sort of persons who think that new truths may have been desirable once, but that we have had enough of them now.", rather than S1, "A theory which maintains that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm, cannot be charged with being intentionally hostile to the reception of new truths; but we cannot commend the generosity of its dealing with the persons to whom mankind are indebted for them."?
